 - speech: the Drama 3 backbone's full attention runs the reference's softmax schedule: one running max per 64-key tile, with the reference kernel's exp, division, gate sigmoid and fused multiply-adds. On our GB10, the attention operator now matches the reference on 98.1% and 96.2% of its outputs at layers 3 and 39 (was 70.4% and 57.5%), and on our 182-token text fixture the backbone's distance to the reference fell from 0.124 to 0.053 relative L2 (semantic KL 0.024 to 0.0062, worst row cosine 0.80 to 0.92). Rows keep their bits whatever the chunk size, and decode uses the same arithmetic.
 - speech: a streamed Drama 3 reply that collapses into silence now ends at the same frame as the whole reply of the same generation: the frame that completes 10 s of silence. Before, on our GB10 with a test reply, the stream ended 12.7 s later, because the silence guard read each chunk after the stream's client had begun converting it to PCM16 in place. The guard now hears each chunk before it is handed over ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md#silence-guard))
 - speech: a Drama 3 synthesis that stays silent (below -60 dBFS) for 10 s ends there with `X-Finish-Reason: silence` and keeps its audio. Before, a collapsed reply generated up to a minute of near-digital silence before its end of speech or the frame limit. A stall that recovers sooner (one measured stall lasted 7.4 s) is kept untouched. Silence reached in the last frames still counts, ahead of the end of speech and the frame limit. With `--long-form`, such a segment ends the reply. To hear the audio while generating, a whole reply now decodes every 16 frames, with the incremental decode streams already use. On the 48-request default-voice capture, all 46 normal replies were byte-identical to the previous build, and the reply that stayed silent for 37 s was cut 10 s into its stall, a byte-identical prefix ([docs](docs/drama-voices.md#silence-guard))
